WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 198: Barack Obama: Dreams from My Father, the 2004 Keynote, and Hope (1961–2008)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.880
[Rebecca] Today we step into the historic, barrier-breaking world of Barack Obama.

4
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:22.880
[Rebecca] With Obama, the American presidency achieved an extraordinary milestone, fulfilling the promises of the civil rights movement through the power of soaring oratory, community organization, and unshakable hope.

5
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:33.280
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the boy from Honolulu, the 24 Boston Convention speech, and the historic night in Grant Park.

6
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.200
[Rebecca] Barack Hussein Obama I.

7
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:52.960
[George] A brilliant, charismatic economics student from the shores of Lake Victoria in Kenya, and Anne Dunham, a free-spirited, intellectual anthropologist from Wichita, Kansas.

8
00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:07.1000
[George] When his father departed for Harvard and returned to Kenya, young Bari was raised by his mother and his beloved maternal grandparents, Stanley and Madeline Dunham, affectionately known as Gramps and Toot in a modest apartment near Punahou School.

9
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:24.160
[George] For four formative childhood years, Bari lived in Jakarta, Indonesia, with his mother and Indonesian stepfather Lolo Sotoro, running through tropical rains, keeping baby crocodiles as pets, and witnessing the brutal realities of third world poverty.

10
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:34.320
[George] Returning to Honolulu, the young boy wrestled with his complex multiracial identity, devouring books by James Baldwin, Ralph Ellison, and W.E.B.

11
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.320
[George] Du Boys while shooting hoops on the asphalt courts of Honolulu.

12
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:47.840
[George] His mother taught him that every human life possesses equal nobility, instilling in him a boundless empathy that would become his defining political superpower.

13
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] Growing up across Hawaii and Indonesia gave Barack Obama a unique, global perspective on human dignity, empathy, and the interconnectedness of all cultures.

14
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:06.280
[George] His multicultural upbringing gave Obama an instinctive understanding of the diverse threads that form the rich fabric of American society.

15
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:14.440
[George] His unique life journey across two oceans gave Barack Obama an instinctive faith in the universal human longing for freedom and equality.

16
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:23.240
[George] Graduating from Columbia University in 1983, 24-year-old Obama made a choice that mystified his ambitious classmates.

17
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:54.760
[George] He turned down lucrative corporate job offers in Manhattan and moved to Chicago to work as a community organizer for the developing communities project for $12,000 a year, driving a rusted-out blue Honda civic through the impoverished steel mill neighborhoods of the far south side, Obama worked with local African American church pastors to set up job training programs, establish tutoring centers, and force city authorities to remove toxic asbestos from the Altgeld Gardens public housing projects.

18
00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] In 1988, seeking the intellectual tools to wield larger systemic power, Obama entered Harvard Law School.

19
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:13.320
[George] In February 1990, Obama made national headlines when he was elected the first African American president of the prestigious Harvard Law Review in its 104 year history.

20
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:30.920
[George] Celebrated for his extraordinary ability to bridge the bitter ideological divides between conservative and liberal editors, Obama graduated Magna cum laude, returning to Chicago to direct Project Vote, registering 150,000 new voters for the 1992 election.

21
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:40.920
[George] His work on the South Side of Chicago taught him the power of grassroots civic organization, proving that ordinary citizens can transform their communities from the bottom up.

22
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:49.040
[George] His years as an organizer taught him that true democratic power comes from organized, informed citizens working together for the common good.

23
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:57.840
[George] Organizing on the South Side of Chicago gave him an unshakable connection to the struggles of everyday working families striving for a better life.

24
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:05.840
[George] In 2004, after eight productive years serving in the Illinois State Senate, Obama ran for the United States Senate.

25
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:19.520
[George] When Democratic presidential nominee John Kerry invited the relatively unknown 42-year-old state senator to deliver the keynote address at the Democratic National Convention in Boston, few outside Illinois knew his name.

26
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:28.880
[George] On the evening of July 27, 2004, Obama strode to the podium and delivered one of the greatest political addresses in American history.

27
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:37.440
[George] Speaking of his Kenyan father and Kansas mother, he declared, I stand here knowing that my story is part of the larger American story.

28
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:44.480
[George] That I owe a debt to all of those who came before me, and that in no other country on earth is my story even possible.

29
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.440
[George] Then came the electric passage that shattered the cynical narrative of a divided republic.

30
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.960
[George] There's not a liberal America and a conservative America.

31
00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.520
[George] There's the United States of America.

32
00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.720
[George] There's not a black America and white America and Latino America and Asian America.

33
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.040
[George] There's the United States of America.

34
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.520
[George] By the time he finished, an overnight political sensation was born.

35
00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:19.200
[George] That electrifying keynote speech in Boston dismantled the cynical divide of red states and blue states, articulating an inspiring vision of national unity that captivated the world.

36
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:28.000
[George] That speech in Boston launched a political movement that would transform American politics, reminding citizens of the sacred unity of the Republic.

37
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:36.840
[George] That historic speech in Boston called the nation back to its founding promise, proving that what unites Americans is far greater than what divides them.

38
00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:56.760
[George] In February 2007, standing in freezing 20-degree weather on the steps of the old state capitol in Springfield, Illinois, the very place where Abraham Lincoln had delivered his famous, house divided speech, 45-year-old Senator Barack Obama announced his audacious candidacy for the presidency.

39
00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:08.840
[George] He faced the formidable political juggernaut of New York Senator Hillary Roddam Clinton, who was backed by the party establishment, massive financial war chests, and universal name recognition.

40
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:23.720
[George] Led by brilliant campaign manager David Plough and chief strategist David Axelrod, Obama mounted a revolutionary grassroots campaign fueled by digital organizing, small donations, and an unquenchable message of hope and change.

41
00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:35.960
[George] In the snowy Iowa caucuses in January 2008, Obama achieved an astonishing upset victory, carried by thousands of young, first-time voters chanting, Yes we can.

42
00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:44.360
[George] In a grueling, 17-month primary duel that crisscrossed 50 states, Obama outmaneuvered the Clinton machine delegate by delegate.

43
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.280
[George] Securing the Democratic nomination in June 2008.

44
00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:59.960
[George] The Audacity of Hope campaign inspired millions of young people to participate in the democratic process for the first time, proving that genuine idealism can overcome established political machines.

45
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:09.240
[George] The historic primary victory showed the power of grassroots organization, digital campaigning, and an unshakable message of democratic hope.

46
00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] The 2008 campaign showed that an authentic grassroots movement animated by hope and civic duty can triumph over conventional political cynicism.

47
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:37.840
[George] In March 2008, Obama's presidential campaign faced an existential crisis when inflammatory video clips surfaced of his former pastor, the Reverend Jeremiah Wright of Trinity United Church of Christ, making incendiary statements regarding American foreign policy and racial injustice.

48
00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.320
[George] Pundits declared the campaign doomed.

49
00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:52.400
[George] Rather than releasing an evasive political soundbite or dismissing his pastor with cynical coldness, Obama chose to confront the painful complexities of race with unprecedented intellectual honesty.

50
00:07:52.400 --> 00:08:02.640
[George] On March 18, 2008, at the National Constitution Center in Philadelphia, Obama delivered an epical address titled, A More Perfect Union.

51
00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:13.440
[George] Speaking of his white grandmother who had feared young black men on the street, and his black pastor who harbored the anger of a segregated generation, Obama refused to disown either.

52
00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.680
[George] Explaining that their complexities were the complexities of America itself.

53
00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:30.480
[George] He called upon citizens to work together to heal the original sin of slavery and fulfill the promise of the Constitution, turning a political crisis into a profound moment of national dialogue and moral leadership.

54
00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:41.280
[George] His profound address on race in Philadelphia stood as an act of courageous intellectual honesty, inviting the American people into an honest dialogue on historical injustice.

55
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:50.240
[George] The Philadelphia speech was hailed as a rhetorical masterpiece, elevating the political discourse and confronting the nation's deepest racial wounds.

56
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] His Philadelphia address on race elevated the national discourse, inviting citizens into an honest, courageous conversation about historical healing.

57
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:10.840
[George] In the autumn of 2008, as the global financial system shuddered under the collapse of Wall Street, Obama challenged Republican nominee Senator John McCain of Arizona.

58
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:26.840
[George] Radiating cool, unflappable composure, earning the affectionate moniker, No Drama Obama, Obama selected experienced Delaware Senator Joe Biden as his running mate, contrasting his steady economic plan with the chaotic atmosphere of the financial panic.

59
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:33.960
[George] On Tuesday, November 4, 2008, the American electorate delivered a historic verdict.

60
00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:44.680
[George] Barack Obama won nearly 69.5 million votes, the highest total in American history up to that date, carrying 365 electoral votes.

61
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:50.760
[George] Winning traditional Republican bastions like Virginia, North Carolina, and Indiana.

62
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:59.080
[George] 143 years after the abolition of slavery, the United States of America had elected its first African American president.

63
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:09.880
[George] The historic victory of November 4, 2008, fulfilled the dreams of generations of civil rights heroes who had marched, bled, and died for the promise of equality.

64
00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:18.520
[George] The election of the first black president was celebrated across the globe as a triumphant milestone in the universal human struggle for civil rights.

65
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:27.160
[George] That historic election night shattered barriers of prejudice that had stood for centuries, fulfilling the highest promises of the American founding.

66
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:38.040
[George] On election night, November 4, 2008, over 240,000 joyful citizens assembled in Grant Park in downtown Chicago under the autumn stars.

67
00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:54.960
[George] Millions around the world wept in awe, including civil rights pioneer Jesse Jackson, who stood in the crowd with tears pouring down his face, and 90-year-old African Americans who had grown up in the segregated Jim Crow South and never believed they would live to see this day.

68
00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:03.520
[George] At 11 o'clock, Barack, Michelle, Malia, and Sasha Obama walked out onto the floodlit stage behind bulletproof glass.

69
00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:09.360
[George] Looking out over the ocean of American flags, Obama spoke words of transcendent poetry.

70
00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:21.760
[George] If there is anyone out there who still doubts that America is a place where all things are possible, who still wonders if the dream of our founders is alive in our time, who still questions the power of our democracy.

71
00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.520
[George] Tonight is your answer.

72
00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:33.920
[George] He paid tribute to 106-year-old Anne Nixon Cooper, the daughter of slaves who had cast her ballot in Atlanta, reminding the nation that change had come to America.

73
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:42.560
[George] The historic triumph reaffirmed that the American Democratic Experiment remained an ongoing, miraculous journey toward a more perfect union.

74
00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:53.280
[George] That unforgettable evening in Grant Park was a glorious affirmation that the American Democratic Experiment remains an ongoing, miraculous journey toward a more perfect union.

75
00:11:53.280 --> 00:12:02.480
[George] That magical night in Grant Park reminded the world that America remains a land of boundless possibility where the founding ideals are alive and true.

76
00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:12.320
[George] Grant Park was an unforgettable celebration of democratic renewal, proving that in America, ordinary citizens possess the power to change history.

77
00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:21.000
[Rebecca] Shattered a 200 year racial barrier and inspired millions around the globe.

78
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:33.160
[Rebecca] But entering the Oval Office in the midst of the Great Recession, he faced titanic legislative battles, the raid on Osama bin Laden, and the complex currents of 21st century democracy.

79
00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:40.600
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our entire 90 episode saga presidents with episode 199.

80
00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:45.080
[Rebecca] The modern presidency from Obama through Donald Trump and Joe Biden.

81
00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:46.760
[Rebecca] The Resilient Republic.

82
00:12:51.160 --> 00:13:02.760
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

